				\begin{document}$$ {\text{q}}_{{{\text{CO}}_{2} }} $$\end{document}qCO2 (second peak) and remained limited throughout the fermentation, especially on glycerol (Fig. 1). Lipid contents of Y. lipolytica cells (as % of dry weight) increased significantly with glycerol as the carbon source and reached 40% compared to 23% with glucose. Indeed, the requirement of glycerol as a direct precursor for triacylglycerides (TAG) might explain such results. Glycerol as favored C-source for lipid accumulation was also recently observed in Rhodosporidium toruloides [29, 35].Fig. 1
Biomass, products formation, CO2 production rate and substrate consumption in a pO2 uncontrolled batch culture of Y. lipolytica grown on glucose (a) or glycerol (b) as the sole carbon source
